<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">One Token Per Frame: Reconsidering Visual Bandwidth
in World Models for VLA Policy</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Zuojin Tang
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Zhejiang University
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Shengchao Yuan
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Central South University
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Xiaoxin Bai
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Harbin Institute of Technology
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Zhiyuan Jin
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>E-surfing Digital Life Technology Co., Ltd., China Telecom<sup id="id1" class="ltx_sup">*</sup>Corresponding Author
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">De Ma
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Zhejiang University
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Gang Pan
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Bin Liu
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author"><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Embodied Intelligence General Platform Laboratory, Chery Auto
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Vision-language-action (VLA) models increasingly rely on auxiliary world modules to plan over long horizons, yet how such modules should be parameterized on top of a pretrained VLA remains an open design question. Existing world-model-augmented VLAs typically pass the per-frame visual stream into the world module at high visual bandwidth and treat its rollout as a side product of action prediction; under a constrained adaptation budget on a frozen backbone, this leaves both the per-frame representation and the latent action coupling under-examined. We introduce <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">OneWM-VLA</span>, which compresses each view into a single semantic token per frame through an Adaptive Attention Pooling, and produces the resulting latent stream and the action trajectory under a single flow-matching objective rather than connecting them through a separate decoder. Empirically, we find that per-frame visual bandwidth can be reduced to a single token without compromising long-horizon performance under our setup. Trained with <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="14.71" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>14.71</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">14.71</annotation></semantics></math>M LoRA parameters on a <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (2B) backbone, OneWM-VLA improves the average success rate from <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="47.9\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>47.9</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">47.9\%</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="61.3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>61.3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">61.3\%</annotation></semantics></math> on MetaWorld MT50, reaches <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="95.6\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>95.6</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">95.6\%</annotation></semantics></math> on LIBERO-Long (vs. <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="85.2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>85.2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">85.2\%</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>), and reaches <math id="abstract1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="60.0\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>60.0</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">60.0\%</annotation></semantics></math> on the long-horizon deformable task <em id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Fold Cloth</em> on a real Piper arm (vs. <math id="abstract1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20.0\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>20.0</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20.0\%</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="abstract1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Vision-language-action (VLA) models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brohan et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zitkovich et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chi et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Black et al. (2026)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Intelligence et al. (2025)</a></cite> have become a dominant paradigm for robot learning, mapping language instructions and visual observations directly to actions. While such policies excel at short-horizon imitation, they fall short on tasks that require anticipating how the scene will change under future actions, leading to error accumulation along the planning horizon. A natural remedy is to attach a forward model of environment dynamics to the policy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cen et al. (2025b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2025b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2024)</a></cite>, predicting future video frames as auxiliary supervision or as goals for action generation. While future-frame prediction is an expressive grounding signal, the per-step compute of pixel-level rollouts grows quickly with the horizon, and the prediction target is largely dominated by photometric detail rather than control-relevant dynamics, making it costly to scale when only a small adaptation budget is available on top of a frozen backbone.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Latent-space world models offer a complementary route. Recurrent state-space models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hafner et al. (2019)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hafner et al. (2025)</a></cite> and joint-embedding predictors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">LeCun (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bardes et al. (2024)</a></cite> show that compact learned representations support long-horizon reasoning at much lower per-step cost than pixel-level rollouts, by predicting in a learned representation rather than in pixel space. These methods have, however, largely been developed within their own reinforcement learning or self-supervised pipelines, and have not been carried over to the adaptation regime of a pretrained generative VLA, where the backbone is largely frozen and only a small set of additional parameters is trained. This raises a basic design question for world-module-augmented VLAs in this regime: how much per-frame visual bandwidth a world module actually needs while still supporting long-horizon control, and how the resulting latent stream should be coupled to action generation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<p id="S1.F1.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center ltx_align_center"><span id="S1.F1.2.1" class="ltx_text"><img src="2605.07931v1/motivation_new.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/68;" width="476" height="68" alt="Refer to caption"></span></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Motivation. OneWM-VLA represents each frame by a single semantic latent, keeping the per-step world-module budget horizon-invariant.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we propose OneWM-VLA (Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ One Token Per Frame: Reconsidering Visual Bandwidth in World Models for VLA Policy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), a world-module-augmented VLA that instantiates the two design choices above as a bottleneck–rollout coupling. A lightweight Adaptive Attention Pooling distills each view into a single semantic latent token per frame, so that per-frame visual bandwidth is held at one token and the per-step world-module budget remains horizon-invariant. A joint flow-matching objective then generates the future latent stream and the future action trajectory under one model, so the predicted latent stream serves as a structural prior on the action trajectory rather than a side channel produced by a separate decoder. The bottleneck makes the joint objective tractable across the horizon, and the joint objective gives the bottleneck a control-relevant supervisory signal. We instantiate OneWM-VLA on top of the pretrained <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Black et al. (2026)</a></cite> and train it with LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2022)</a></cite>; at inference, only the action branch is executed on the robot, while the latent tokens provide an internal auxiliary trajectory that helps structure action generation over the horizon.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate OneWM-VLA on simulated benchmarks and a real Piper arm. On MetaWorld MT50, OneWM-VLA improves the average success rate from <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="47.9\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>47.9</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">47.9\%</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="61.3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>61.3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">61.3\%</annotation></semantics></math>; on LIBERO-Long, it reaches <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="95.6\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>95.6</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">95.6\%</annotation></semantics></math>, above the <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="85.2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>85.2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">85.2\%</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. On the real Piper arm, it reaches <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="60.0\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>60.0</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">60.0\%</annotation></semantics></math> on the long-horizon deformable task <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Fold Cloth</em>, compared with <math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20.0\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>20.0</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20.0\%</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. To probe the design choice itself, we sweep the per-frame visual bandwidth from <math id="S1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math> tokens under a matched training budget; counter to the intuition that more visual tokens should help, success rate decreases monotonically as the bandwidth grows. This is consistent with the view that, in this adaptation regime, per-frame visual bandwidth is not where the difficulty primarily lies, and that a single semantic token per frame is a reasonable operating point rather than a degenerate one.
Our contributions are as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We identify that pixel-level world modules scale poorly with the planning horizon when added on top of a pretrained VLA, and find that under our setup the per-frame visual bandwidth can be reduced to a single semantic token without compromising long-horizon control.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce OneWM-VLA, which realizes the <em id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">bottleneck–rollout coupling</em> through a single-token-per-frame Adaptive Attention Pooling and a joint flow-matching objective that generates the future latent stream and the action trajectory under one model.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We report consistent gains over the <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> backbone on LIBERO, MetaWorld MT50, and a real Piper arm, supported by a per-frame bandwidth sweep and a latent-supervision ablation in the adaptation regime studied here.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Vision-Language-Action Models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Driven by the rapid progress of Multimodal Large Language Models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Beyer et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al. (2025b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al. (2025a)</a></cite> and the emergence of large-scale robot datasets, Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brohan et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zitkovich et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Intelligence et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Black et al. (2026)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tang et al. (2025b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qu et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bu et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goyal et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shukor et al. (2025)</a></cite> have become a dominant paradigm in robot learning, fine-tuning multimodal LLMs to map language instructions and visual observations directly to actions. To better capture the multi-modal nature of robot actions, a line of work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chi et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Black et al. (2026)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Intelligence et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2024)</a></cite> replaces deterministic decoding with diffusion or flow matching. A complementary line of work extends VLAs with chain-of-thought reasoning, auxiliary heads, or multimodal traces <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeng et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2024)</a></cite>, which strengthens short-horizon planning. However, such policies remain largely reactive: they map current observations to actions without modeling how the scene will change under those actions, leaving long-horizon error accumulation unaddressed. In contrast, our work attaches an explicit forward model to the VLA, so the policy can anticipate future scene evolution while generating actions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>World Models for Policy Learning</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Equipping policies with a forward model of environment dynamics has been a long-standing direction in model-based control. One line of work predicts in pixel space: autoregressive transformers or diffusion models synthesize future frames as goals or auxiliary supervision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cen et al. (2025b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bjorck et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheang et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhen et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jang et al. (2025)</a></cite>, and recent methods jointly generate future video and actions within a single framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2025a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cen et al. (2025a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2025b)</a></cite>. A complementary line of work predicts in a learned latent space: recurrent state-space models such as Dreamer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hafner et al. (2019)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hafner et al. (2025)</a></cite> roll out imagined trajectories in a low-dimensional posterior, JEPA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">LeCun (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bardes et al. (2024)</a></cite> predicts in a learned representation rather than in pixels, and sequence-modeling formulations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tang et al. (2025a)</a></cite> show that compact latent representations can support long-horizon reasoning at much lower per-step cost. However, pixel-level approaches inherit the burden of dense reconstruction and per-step rollout cost grows quickly with the planning horizon, while latent-space approaches have largely been developed within their own RL or self-supervised pipelines and have not been carried over to a pretrained generative VLA. In contrast, our work attaches a compact latent world module on top of a pretrained VLA, and generates the latent rollout and the action trajectory under a single flow-matching objective rather than as two separate stages.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<p id="S2.F2.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center ltx_align_center"><span id="S2.F2.2.1" class="ltx_text"><img src="2605.07931v1/onewm_vla.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/202;" width="476" height="202" alt="Refer to caption"></span></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>The OneWM-VLA Framework. Through Adaptive Attention Pooling (Adaptive Fusion), we distill multi-view visual features into a dense latent world token for each perspective. These tokens are aligned with action trajectories via Joint Flow Matching to ensure alignment between environment dynamics transitions and robot control.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Flow Matching</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Flow matching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lipman et al. (2022)</a></cite> trains a continuous normalizing flow by regressing a vector field <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> rather than simulating the underlying ODE. Given a probability path <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> that connects the data distribution <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and a prior <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, the objective is</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{FM}}(\theta)\;=\;\mathbb{E}_{t,\;x\sim p_{t}}\!\left[\|v_{\theta}(t,x)-u_{t}(x)\|^{2}\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>FM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{FM}}(\theta)\;=\;\mathbb{E}_{t,\;x\sim p_{t}}\!\left[\|v_{\theta}(t,x)-u_{t}(x)\|^{2}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is the target velocity field. Because <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is in general intractable, we use the conditional flow matching (CFM) objective: with Gaussian conditional paths <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x\mid x_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x\mid x_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and the closed-form conditional fields <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(x\mid x_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(x\mid x_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{CFM}}(\theta)\;=\;\mathbb{E}_{t,\;q(x_{1}),\;p_{t}(x\mid x_{1})}\!\left[\|v_{\theta}(t,x)-u_{t}(x\mid x_{1})\|^{2}\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>CFM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{CFM}}(\theta)\;=\;\mathbb{E}_{t,\;q(x_{1}),\;p_{t}(x\mid x_{1})}\!\left[\|v_{\theta}(t,x)-u_{t}(x\mid x_{1})\|^{2}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">This objective is simulation-free at training time, and is the policy-side training objective inherited from the <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> backbone.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We instantiate the bottleneck–rollout coupling introduced in Sec. <a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction ‣ One Token Per Frame: Reconsidering Visual Bandwidth in World Models for VLA Policy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> as two interdependent components (Fig. <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.2 World Models for Policy Learning ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ One Token Per Frame: Reconsidering Visual Bandwidth in World Models for VLA Policy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). The bottleneck (Sec. <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Per-Frame Compression via Adaptive Attention Pooling ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ One Token Per Frame: Reconsidering Visual Bandwidth in World Models for VLA Policy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>) reduces each view to a single semantic latent token per frame through an Adaptive Attention Pooling, so that the per-step world-module token budget remains horizon-invariant. The rollout (Sec. <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Joint Flow Matching over Latents and Actions ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ One Token Per Frame: Reconsidering Visual Bandwidth in World Models for VLA Policy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>) generates the future latent stream and the future action trajectory under a single flow-matching objective, so that the predicted latent stream serves as a structural prior on the action sequence rather than a side channel produced by a separate decoder. The bottleneck makes the joint objective tractable across the horizon, and the joint objective gives the bottleneck a control-relevant supervisory signal; we return to their empirical attribution in Sec. <a href="#S5" title="5 Experiments ‣ One Token Per Frame: Reconsidering Visual Bandwidth in World Models for VLA Policy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Per-Frame Compression via Adaptive Attention Pooling</h3>

<figure id="S4.F3.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle ltx_align_floatright" style="width:172.5pt;">
<p id="S4.F3.fig1.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.F3.fig1.2.1" class="ltx_text"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2605.07931v1/onewm_adaptive.svg" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/152;" width="238" height="152"></object></span></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Adaptive attention pooling.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Adaptive Attention Pooling reduces each view to a single token per frame in two stages: a token-level multi-strategy pooling and a view-level adaptive fusion. We process each camera independently and write <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{r,w_{1},w_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{r,w_{1},w_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math> for the third-person view and the two wrist views.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Visual encoding.</span> For each view <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, we extract token features with the pretrained PaliGemma <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Beyer et al. (2024)</a></cite> encoder <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> from the input frames <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{I}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times T\times H\times W\times C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{I}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times T\times H\times W\times C}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{i}\;=\;\mathcal{E}_{\phi}(\mathbf{I}_{i})\;\in\;\mathbb{R}^{B\times T\times N\times D}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{i}\;=\;\mathcal{E}_{\phi}(\mathbf{I}_{i})\;\in\;\mathbb{R}^{B\times T\times N\times D},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N{=}256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N{=}256</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of visual tokens per frame and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is the hidden dimension. We write the <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-th token of view <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{i}^{(n)}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{i}^{(n)}\in\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multi-strategy token pooling.</span> We use three complementary scoring functions <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{m}:\mathbb{R}^{D}\!\to\!\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{m}:\mathbb{R}^{D}\!\to\!\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\mathcal{M}=\{\textsc{Max},\textsc{Sum},\textsc{Learn}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Max</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Sum</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Learn</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\mathcal{M}=\{\textsc{Max},\textsc{Sum},\textsc{Learn}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, that score each token along different notions of saliency,</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\textsc{Max}}(\mathbf{x})\,=\,\max_{d}\,x^{(d)},\quad\phi_{\textsc{Sum}}(\mathbf{x})\,=\,\sum_{d=1}^{D}x^{(d)},\quad\phi_{\textsc{Learn}}(\mathbf{x})\,=\,Q_{\theta}(\mathbf{x})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Max</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.448em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>d</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Sum</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>D</mi></munderover><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Learn</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.448em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\textsc{Max}}(\mathbf{x})\,=\,\max_{d}\,x^{(d)},\quad\phi_{\textsc{Sum}}(\mathbf{x})\,=\,\sum_{d=1}^{D}x^{(d)},\quad\phi_{\textsc{Learn}}(\mathbf{x})\,=\,Q_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">corresponding to the peak channel response, the total channel response, and a learned task-aware response from a small view-specific MLP <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each scoring function induces a softmax distribution over the <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> tokens at temperature <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{m}^{(n)}\;=\;\frac{\exp\!\bigl(\phi_{m}(\mathbf{x}_{i}^{(n)})/\tau\bigr)}{\sum_{j=1}^{N}\exp\!\bigl(\phi_{m}(\mathbf{x}_{i}^{(j)})/\tau\bigr)},\qquad m\in\mathcal{M}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{m}^{(n)}\;=\;\frac{\exp\!\bigl(\phi_{m}(\mathbf{x}_{i}^{(n)})/\tau\bigr)}{\sum_{j=1}^{N}\exp\!\bigl(\phi_{m}(\mathbf{x}_{i}^{(j)})/\tau\bigr)},\qquad m\in\mathcal{M},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">which is then used to aggregate the <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> tokens along the token axis while keeping the feature dimension <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> intact,</p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}_{\textsc{Max}}\,=\,\max_{n}\bigl(w_{\textsc{Max}}^{(n)}\,\mathbf{x}_{i}^{(n)}\bigr),\qquad\mathbf{p}_{m}\,=\,\sum_{n=1}^{N}w_{m}^{(n)}\,\mathbf{x}_{i}^{(n)},\;\;m\in\{\textsc{Sum},\textsc{Learn}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Max</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.448em" rspace="0.448em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>n</mi></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Max</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.727em">,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Sum</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Learn</mtext><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}_{\textsc{Max}}\,=\,\max_{n}\bigl(w_{\textsc{Max}}^{(n)}\,\mathbf{x}_{i}^{(n)}\bigr),\qquad\mathbf{p}_{m}\,=\,\sum_{n=1}^{N}w_{m}^{(n)}\,\mathbf{x}_{i}^{(n)},\;\;m\in\{\textsc{Sum},\textsc{Learn}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.4" class="ltx_p">This produces three pooled tokens <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{p}_{m}\}_{m\in\mathcal{M}}\subset\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{p}_{m}\}_{m\in\mathcal{M}}\subset\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> per frame, one per scoring strategy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Adaptive view fusion.</span> The three pooled tokens are combined into a single per-frame world token via a learnable convex combination. With trainable scalars <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\alpha}\in\mathbb{R}^{|\mathcal{M}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜶</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\alpha}\in\mathbb{R}^{|\mathcal{M}|}</annotation></semantics></math> and softmax-normalized weights <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜷</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>, the per-view world token <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times T\times 1\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times T\times 1\times D}</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S4.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}_{i}\;=\;\sum_{m\in\mathcal{M}}\beta_{m}\,\mathbf{p}_{m},\qquad\beta_{m}\;=\;\frac{\exp(\alpha_{m}/\tau)}{\sum_{m^{\prime}\in\mathcal{M}}\exp(\alpha_{m^{\prime}}/\tau)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.391em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}_{i}\;=\;\sum_{m\in\mathcal{M}}\beta_{m}\,\mathbf{p}_{m},\qquad\beta_{m}\;=\;\frac{\exp(\alpha_{m}/\tau)}{\sum_{m^{\prime}\in\mathcal{M}}\exp(\alpha_{m^{\prime}}/\tau)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Because each view contributes one token per frame, the world-module token budget per step is independent of the visual resolution <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, and the total token count fed into the joint generator scales as the planning horizon <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> multiplied by the number of views, rather than as <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N{\cdot}H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N{\cdot}H</annotation></semantics></math>. Two sources of adaptation enter the pooling. The token-level weights <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{m}^{(n)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{m}^{(n)}</annotation></semantics></math> depend on the input through <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and vary from frame to frame, providing instance-specific feature selection. The view-level fusion weights <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> are learned scalars that are fixed at inference; we report their learned values in Sec. <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3 Q2: Does the adaptive pooling matter? ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ One Token Per Frame: Reconsidering Visual Bandwidth in World Models for VLA Policy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Joint Flow Matching over Latents and Actions</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The bottleneck above turns each view into a per-frame latent token <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. We now describe how the rollout couples the future latent stream with the action trajectory under a single flow-matching objective, so that the latent stream serves as a structural prior on the action sequence rather than a side channel.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Joint probability path.</span> Let <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\mathbb{R}^{H\times D_{a}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\mathbb{R}^{H\times D_{a}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the future action sequence and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in\mathbb{R}^{H\times D_{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>z</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in\mathbb{R}^{H\times D_{z}}</annotation></semantics></math> the future latent world tokens. Following the optimal-transport (OT) displacement parameterization adopted by <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Black et al. (2026)</a></cite>, we extend the same straight-line interpolation to the latent branch, so that both branches share an identical flow-time schedule. Given data samples <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,z)</annotation></semantics></math> and noise <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon_{a},\epsilon_{z})\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon_{a},\epsilon_{z})\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)</annotation></semantics></math>, the interpolated states at flow time <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> are</p>
<table id="S4.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{a}_{t}\,=\,t\,\epsilon_{a}+(1{-}t)\,a,\qquad x^{z}_{t}\,=\,t\,\epsilon_{z}+(1{-}t)\,z,\qquad t\sim\mathrm{Beta}(1.5,1)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>a</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.448em" rspace="0.448em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>z</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.448em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>z</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Beta</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{a}_{t}\,=\,t\,\epsilon_{a}+(1{-}t)\,a,\qquad x^{z}_{t}\,=\,t\,\epsilon_{z}+(1{-}t)\,z,\qquad t\sim\mathrm{Beta}(1.5,1),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where the <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Beta}(1.5,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Beta</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Beta}(1.5,1)</annotation></semantics></math> schedule places more probability mass near <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t{=}0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t{=}0</annotation></semantics></math>, biasing training toward the data end of the path. The corresponding target velocity fields, which are constant along each interpolation, are</p>
<table id="S4.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{a}_{t}\,=\,\epsilon_{a}-a,\qquad u^{z}_{t}\,=\,\epsilon_{z}-z." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>a</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.448em" rspace="0.448em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>z</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.448em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{a}_{t}\,=\,\epsilon_{a}-a,\qquad u^{z}_{t}\,=\,\epsilon_{z}-z.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multi-objective flow-matching loss.</span> The transformer input is organized as a fused sequence interleaving current tokens <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x^{z_{r}}_{t},\,x^{z_{w_{1}}}_{t},\,x^{z_{w_{2}}}_{t},\,l_{t},\,s_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msubsup><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></msubsup><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></msubsup><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x^{z_{r}}_{t},\,x^{z_{w_{1}}}_{t},\,x^{z_{w_{2}}}_{t},\,l_{t},\,s_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math> (third-person view <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, wrist views <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{1},w_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1},w_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, language <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>, robot state <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>) with future noisy queries <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x^{z_{r}}_{t+k},\,x^{z_{w_{1}}}_{t+k},\,x^{z_{w_{2}}}_{t+k},\,x^{a}_{t+k}\}_{k=1}^{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></msubsup><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></msubsup><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></msubsup><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mi>a</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>h</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x^{z_{r}}_{t+k},\,x^{z_{w_{1}}}_{t+k},\,x^{z_{w_{2}}}_{t+k},\,x^{a}_{t+k}\}_{k=1}^{h}</annotation></semantics></math>. The model <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> predicts a velocity field for both branches, trained with</p>
<table id="S4.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}\;=\;\lambda_{a}\,\mathbb{E}\!\left[\|v^{a}_{\theta}-u^{a}_{t}\|_{1}\right]\;+\;\sum_{i\in\{r,w_{1},w_{2}\}}\!\lambda_{i}\,\mathbb{E}\!\left[\|v^{z}_{\theta}-u^{z}_{t}\|_{1}\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi>𝔼</mi></mpadded><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>a</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>a</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.280em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.335em">+</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi>𝔼</mi></mpadded><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>z</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>z</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}\;=\;\lambda_{a}\,\mathbb{E}\!\left[\|v^{a}_{\theta}-u^{a}_{t}\|_{1}\right]\;+\;\sum_{i\in\{r,w_{1},w_{2}\}}\!\lambda_{i}\,\mathbb{E}\!\left[\|v^{z}_{\theta}-u^{z}_{t}\|_{1}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Because the latent and action branches share the same generator and the same flow time, their predicted velocities <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{z}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>z</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{z}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{a}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>a</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{a}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> co-evolve through self-attention during both training and ODE-based sampling, rather than being connected by a post-hoc auxiliary loss. We examine the role of this coupling through a controlled ablation in Sec. <a href="#S5.SS5" title="5.5 Q4: Does the joint generation matter, beyond added capacity? ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ One Token Per Frame: Reconsidering Visual Bandwidth in World Models for VLA Policy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>.At inference, all modalities are initialized from Gaussian noise and jointly denoised, with only the action stream executed on the robot.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate OneWM-VLA on simulated benchmarks and a real Piper arm, organized around two questions that follow from the design described in Sec. <a href="#S4" title="4 Methodology ‣ One Token Per Frame: Reconsidering Visual Bandwidth in World Models for VLA Policy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>: whether the per-frame visual bandwidth can in fact be reduced to a single semantic token under our setup, and how each ingredient of the bottleneck–rollout coupling contributes to the resulting performance. We address these questions through five experimental probes:</p>
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q1</span> (Sec. <a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Q1: Is a single per-frame token enough? ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ One Token Per Frame: Reconsidering Visual Bandwidth in World Models for VLA Policy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>): Is a single per-frame token enough for long-horizon control?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q2</span> (Sec. <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3 Q2: Does the adaptive pooling matter? ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ One Token Per Frame: Reconsidering Visual Bandwidth in World Models for VLA Policy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>): Does the adaptive design of the pooling matter?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q3</span> (Sec. <a href="#S5.SS4" title="5.4 Q3: Does it help to predict in semantic space rather than in pixel space? ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ One Token Per Frame: Reconsidering Visual Bandwidth in World Models for VLA Policy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>): Should compression happen in semantic or in pixel space?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q4</span> (Sec. <a href="#S5.SS5" title="5.5 Q4: Does the joint generation matter, beyond added capacity? ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ One Token Per Frame: Reconsidering Visual Bandwidth in World Models for VLA Policy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>): How much of the gain can be attributed to the joint flow-matching objective rather than to the added latent capacity alone?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q5</span> (Sec. <a href="#S5.SS6" title="5.6 Q5: How does the advantage scale with the planning horizon? ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ One Token Per Frame: Reconsidering Visual Bandwidth in World Models for VLA Policy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.6</span></a>): How does OneWM-VLA scale with the planning horizon, and where does its advantage become most pronounced?</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We treat all experimental conclusions as scoped to the training setup and manipulation suites considered, and refrain from extrapolating beyond them. Tab. <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ One Token Per Frame: Reconsidering Visual Bandwidth in World Models for VLA Policy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, Tab. <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.1 Main Results ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ One Token Per Frame: Reconsidering Visual Bandwidth in World Models for VLA Policy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, and Tab. <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.1 Main Results ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ One Token Per Frame: Reconsidering Visual Bandwidth in World Models for VLA Policy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> report the main numbers; Q1–Q5 then dissect where these numbers come from.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<p id="S5.F4.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.F4.2.1" class="ltx_text"><img src="2605.07931v1/experiment.png" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/118;" width="476" height="118" alt="Refer to caption"></span></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Evaluation suites used in this work: the LIBERO and MetaWorld MT50 simulation benchmarks, and a real Piper robot arm.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Main results on MetaWorld MT50. Success rates (%) across difficulty tiers and planning horizons <math id="S5.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S5.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\pi_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\pi_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\pi_{0.5}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0.5</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\pi_{0.5}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the absolute gain over <math id="S5.T1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.T1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0.5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0.5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0.5}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
<table id="S5.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Method</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Horizon</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Easy</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Medium</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Hard</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Very Hard</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Avg%</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="S5.T1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\pi_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">π</mi><mn mathsize="0.800em">0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\pi_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="S5.T1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\pi_{0.5}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em" mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">π</mi><mn mathsize="0.800em">0.5</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\pi_{0.5}}</annotation></semantics></math></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S5.T1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Diff. Policy</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S5.T1.2.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5</span></th>
<td id="S5.T1.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S5.T1.2.2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8.21</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><span id="S5.T1.2.2.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0.00</span></td>
