<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Q2: Does the adaptive pooling matter?</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Q1 showed that, within the regimes we evaluate, a single per-frame
token is the most effective design point we found. This leaves open
whether <em id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">how</em> that single token is produced matters, since any
reasonable compression to one token might behave similarly, in which
case the adaptive design would not be where the gain comes from. We
look at this question at two levels, first comparing adaptive pooling
against simpler alternatives at the module level, then asking whether
each branch inside the adaptive pooling is actually needed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Module level.</span> Tab. <a href="#S5.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 5.2 Q1: Is a single per-frame token enough? ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ One Token Per Frame: Reconsidering Visual Bandwidth in World Models for VLA Policy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> compares the
full design with two simpler variants: replacing adaptive pooling by
static average pooling, and removing the fusion logic entirely.
Static average pooling drops average success by <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20.5</annotation></semantics></math> pts, with the
largest gap on the Long suite (<math id="S5.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="41.4\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>41.4</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">41.4\%</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="85.0\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>85.0</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">85.0\%</annotation></semantics></math>). Removing the
fusion logic causes a much larger drop, to <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="47.6\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>47.6</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">47.6\%</annotation></semantics></math> on average and
<math id="S5.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.4\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4.4</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.4\%</annotation></semantics></math> on Long. Within this controlled comparison,
input-dependent attention, rather than the act of compression itself,
is what preserves task-relevant structure under aggressive
compression.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Branch level.</span> We then ask whether all three branches inside
adaptive pooling are needed. Tab. <a href="#S5.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 5.2 Q1: Is a single per-frame token enough? ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ One Token Per Frame: Reconsidering Visual Bandwidth in World Models for VLA Policy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> reports
the result on MetaWorld. Each branch on its own is suboptimal:
<span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Learn</span> reaches <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50.42\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>50.42</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50.42\%</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Sum</span> reaches <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="55.19\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>55.19</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">55.19\%</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Max</span> collapses on Hard tasks at <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.0\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.0</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.0\%</annotation></semantics></math>. Combining all three
reaches <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="61.30\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>61.30</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">61.30\%</annotation></semantics></math> and is the most stable across difficulty tiers,
consistent with the three branches capturing complementary aspects of
the same view. After training, the fusion weights settle in
<math id="S5.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\textsc{Learn}}\approx 0.48\text{--}0.57" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Learn</mtext></msub><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>0.48</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>–</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0.57</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\textsc{Learn}}\approx 0.48\text{--}0.57</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S5.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\textsc{Max}}\approx 0.20\text{--}0.28" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Max</mtext></msub><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>0.20</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>–</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0.28</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\textsc{Max}}\approx 0.20\text{--}0.28</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S5.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{\textsc{Sum}}\approx 0.20\text{--}0.28" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Sum</mtext></msub><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>0.20</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>–</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0.28</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{\textsc{Sum}}\approx 0.20\text{--}0.28</annotation></semantics></math>, and remain fixed at
inference, which gives stable and interpretable behavior.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Taken together, the adaptive design, both at the module level and at
the branch level, is what makes single-token compression viable. A
natural follow-up, which we address next, is whether this compression
should happen in a learned semantic space or directly in pixel space.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4 </span>Q3: Does it help to predict in semantic space rather than in pixel space?</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Once compression is shown to be necessary, the next question is
whether it should happen in a learned semantic space or in pixel
space. To answer this, we construct OneWM-VLA-pixel, a variant that
compresses raw visual features into a single token without coupling
to the policy, and evaluate it on MetaWorld MT50 at <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H{=}30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H{=}30</annotation></semantics></math>.
Tab. <a href="#S5.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ 5.4 Q3: Does it help to predict in semantic space rather than in pixel space? ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ One Token Per Frame: Reconsidering Visual Bandwidth in World Models for VLA Policy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> (left) shows that semantic
compression reaches <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="53.13\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>53.13</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">53.13\%</annotation></semantics></math>, against <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="35.85\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>35.85</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">35.85\%</annotation></semantics></math> for pixel
compression, a gap of <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+17.28" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>17.28</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+17.28</annotation></semantics></math> pts, with the largest difference on
Hard tasks (<math id="S5.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40.0\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>40.0</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40.0\%</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="22.0\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>22.0</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">22.0\%</annotation></semantics></math>). A plausible reading is that
pixel-level compression treats task-relevant and task-irrelevant
patterns uniformly and tends to retain low-level noise, whereas
semantic compression operates in a feature space already aligned with
the policy, which filters out much of that noise. The throughput
numbers are consistent with this reading: OneWM-VLA reaches
<math id="S5.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.81" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4.81</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.81</annotation></semantics></math> FPS, against <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.16</annotation></semantics></math> FPS for the pixel baseline
(Tab. <a href="#A6.T14" title="Table 14 ‣ Appendix F Throughput and Memory Analysis ‣ One Token Per Frame: Reconsidering Visual Bandwidth in World Models for VLA Policy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>), since the latter requires additional decoding.
Within this controlled comparison, predicting in semantic space,
rather than in pixel space, is what makes single-token compression
effective in our setting.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T8" class="ltx_table">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure ltx_flex_table">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
